+## Work And Evidence
+
+1. Compare the selected baseline's accepted contracts, public documentation, and relevant implementation. Resolve any conflict through the existing decision authority before presenting a changed behavior as current.
+2. Separate current native-CLI instructions from frozen MVP material. The existing public Extension page places broad headings and examples after one historical banner. Make their scope clear to readers arriving through direct links. Preserve useful history in the appropriate source rather than carrying competing current explanations.
+3. Verify command examples, package layout, managed state, permissions, update and removal behavior, and recovery descriptions against their defining contracts. Keep accepted specifications, observed implementation, and release status distinct. Do not infer release readiness from implemented commands.
+4. Reconcile CLI-owned references to the renamed Extensions Architecture and its narrower responsibility. Check relevant anchors as well as paths. Update stale CLI-status claims in the top-level documentation where the verified baseline warrants it.
+5. Keep installation and update instructions in user-facing setup documentation. Agent startup context remains focused on using the installed Framework. Preserve manual use, optional tooling, and source-specific authority.
+6. Coordinate embedded-catalogue caveats with the separate catalogue synchronization task. Remove them only when its evidence establishes that they are obsolete.
+7. Check affected links and direct section entry points. Verify changed runnable examples through appropriate local evidence. Record the inspected source and executable identities, results, and limitations; add tests only for a concrete uncovered behavior.
